        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A new tab \"Whiteboard 2\" is opened, showing a blank white canvas.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Mia has joined\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 25 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The student's video tile appears on the right side of the screen. It shows the letter \"K\" in a red circle with the name \"Mia\" underneath.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 267 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 102 seconds] The student is alone in the room. The screen shows a blank whiteboard.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A notification sound plays and a banner \"<Tutor> has joined\" appears. The screen layout changes to show two participant windows, one for the student and one for the tutor.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 291 seconds] A YouTube video titled \"Rounding Numbers and Rounding Decimals - The Easy Way!\" is paused on the screen at timestamp 2:42 / 11:33. The word \"Hundred\" is written in white text on the video. Below it are some scribbles in orange and red. The student's icon disappears from the participant list.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The YouTube video is closed, and the screen switches to a blank white whiteboard labeled \"Whiteboard 2\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] A system message appears at the top of the screen: \"<Tutor> has left\". The tutor's icon is removed from the participant list.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 26 seconds] The screen shows a blank whiteboard. No participants are visible in the room.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: Hi, hi Mia.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 11 seconds] Tutor attempts to speak but is muted.\nTurn 2. STUDENT: Yeah, I can hear you.\nTurn 3. TUTOR: Okay, great. Yeah. So, today... Hey, so how's your week? Like we haven't met for several days.\nTurn 4. STUDENT: Um, it's good.\nTurn 5. TUTOR: It's good? Okay, so, um, I actually, um, I've talked to your mom for a little bit. So are you like joining a kind of, um, summer camp now?\nTurn 6. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds] Tutor shares a document on the screen.\nTurn 6. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 7. TUTOR: Okay. So, so like, um, you're not like having school, right? It's just the camp?\nTurn 8. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 9. TUTOR: Okay, okay, I understand. So, yeah, great. So, um, have you learned anything like this week in the camp or are you like going on a field trip?\nTurn 10. STUDENT: Well, uh, we mostly in summer camp, we, uh, do like crafts and...\nTurn 11. TUTOR: Oh, okay. Wow, um, really good. I think that's um, really interesting camp. So, yeah.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Um, another thing like for today's class, we may, um, we may like continue our session on, um, application of algorithm because I think this thing is something that, um, really closely related to you and I hope that some of my questions may gain support for you. So, so I will give you some, um, questions. So, like you can tell me first what kind of, um, like what kind of, um, arithmetic operations we should use in it or like, or like you have any clue about that? Okay?\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: Okay, great. Um, right now.\nTurn 15. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'Emma has 23 apples...' (start)] A document is shared on the screen with the text: \"Emma has 23 apples. Her friend gives her 15 more. How many apples does Emma have now?\"\nTurn 15. STUDENT: Oh, yeah.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Okay, great. So, yeah, here's my question and can you see it clearly?\nTurn 17. STUDENT: Mhm.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: Okay. So let's see. Um, I think this is kind of questions that they're doing now, right? Like, um, in your school or in your camp?\nTurn 19. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 8 seconds] Student draws a line on the whiteboard.\nStudent draws a short horizontal line on the whiteboard.\nTurn 19. TUTOR: Or is it much like harder than this one?\nTurn 20. STUDENT: Um, we mostly not do this.\nTurn 21. TUTOR: Oh, not do this? So what what kind of um things you do now? Like, um more difficult one or more easier one?\nTurn 22. STUDENT: Or other type? Can you give me an example? Like what things you remember?\nTurn 23. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 5 seconds] Student scribbles on the whiteboard with a black pen.\nTurn 23. STUDENT: Well, we almost done learn nothing of um, this.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: Yeah? You can draw it for me?\nTurn 25. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student erases the scribbles.\nStudent draws a curved line and an arrow shape on the whiteboard.\nTutor writes \"Emma\" in orange on the whiteboard.\nStudent writes \"Emma 23\" on the board and then draws a line and an X over it.\nStudent draws a large circle on the board.\nTutor circles the word \"more.\" in orange and types \"apples\" next to it.\nTutor types \"23+15=\" on the board.\nStudent writes \"38\" on the board.\nTutor pastes a new problem onto the board: \"Ethan read 34 pages on Monday, 27 pages on Tuesday, and 41 pages on Wednesday. How many pages did he read in total?\". Tutor also draws an orange line and a semi-circle on the board.\nTurn 25. STUDENT: We almost done learn like this stuff.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Oh. Oh, so I mean, I mean, are you pointing the question? You mean the question now?\nTurn 27. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 28. TUTOR: Oh, okay.\nTurn 29. STUDENT: Like we didn't do that. Like we do another stuff in the in that school.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: Oh, you're doing other stuff? Um, so I mean like, can you give me an example of of what you do in school?\nTurn 31. STUDENT: We do like crafts and\nTurn 32. TUTOR: Oh.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 34. TUTOR: Okay, okay, I understand. So, so it's like you're not doing actually doing math in the camp, right?\nTurn 35. STUDENT: Not really.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: Okay, I understand. So, yeah. So maybe we'll um, like make some extension on this one and um I'll see like how you um understand the question, okay?\nTurn 37. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 38. TUTOR: Okay, great. So, yeah. Um, okay, so let's read the question first and so, um, Emma has 23 apples. Her friend gives her 15 more. So, how many apples does Emma have now? Okay, so for this kind of question, what do you think we should use?\nTurn 39. STUDENT: Hm. Multiplication?\nTurn 40. TUTOR: Multiplication? Are you sure?\nTurn 41. STUDENT: No, no, no, no. The division.\nTurn 42. TUTOR: Division?\nTurn 43. STUDENT: No. Um, wait, what? Oh, plus, plus, plus.\nTurn 44. TUTOR: Yeah, right. Plus, right? So when you see more, right? And they are like kind of same nouns. So there's 15 more apples, right? Here it means apples. Let me type it here. So it mean apples here, right?\nTurn 45. TUTOR: 15 more apples. So they're pointing the same nouns. So that means we are plussing the 23 and the 15, right?\nTurn 46. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 47. TUTOR: Okay, great. So, um, can you, yeah, can can you tell me like what is the answer for this one? I'll help you to write the um, write the formula here and can you tell me what is 23 plus 15?\nTurn 48. STUDENT: What? Oh, 23 plus 15.\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nPush for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
